## CLAUDEST.STG - the ST-Guide help for ClaudeST.
## Compile with HCP (ST-Guide's compiler, or hcp from th-otto/hypview):
##     make -C help        ->  binary/CLAUDEST.HYP and binary/CLAUDEST.REF
## Plain ASCII only: the Atari character set is the same there.

@database "ClaudeST"
@charset "atarist"
@subject "Communication"
@author "Erez Yaary"
@$VER: CLAUDEST.HYP 1.21 (13.10.2026)
@options "+z -s"
@width 75
@help "Help on help"

## ------------------------------------------------------------------------
@node "Main" "ClaudeST"
@symbol "ClaudeST"

                     @{B}ClaudeST@{b} - claude.ai on the Atari

  A GEM client for claude.ai on the Atari ST, STE, Mega ST/STE, TT and
  Falcon. It works like the claude.ai website: your chats, Search, Code,
  Projects and Artifacts on the left, the open conversation on the
  right, and replies streaming in as Claude writes them.

  @{U}Contents@{u}

    @{"Getting started" link "Getting started"}
    @{"The window" link "The window"}
    @{"The status area" link "The status area"}
    @{"Chats, projects and search" link "Chats, projects and search"}
    @{"Right-click menus" link "Right-click menus"}
    @{"Model and effort" link "Model and effort"}
    @{"Code: Claude Code sessions" link "Code sessions"}
    @{"Artifacts" link "Artifacts"}
    @{"Account, plan and usage" link "Account, plan and usage"}
    @{"Hebrew" link "Hebrew"}
    @{"Font" link "Font"}
    @{"Keys" link "Keys"}
    @{"Settings (CLAUDEST.INF)" link "Settings"}
    @{"What is stored where" link "What is stored where"}
    @{"Troubleshooting" link "Troubleshooting"}
    @{"About ClaudeST" link "About ClaudeST"}

  ClaudeST is an unofficial client, not affiliated with Anthropic or
  Atari. Claude is a trademark of Anthropic PBC.
@endnode

## ------------------------------------------------------------------------
@node "Getting started"
@{U}Getting started@{u}

  An 8 MHz 68000 can't do the encryption claude.ai needs, so a small
  program, the @{B}bridge@{b}, runs on a Raspberry Pi (or any Linux
  computer) on your network and talks to claude.ai for the Atari:

    Atari (CLAUDEST.PRG) <-- STinG or serial --> Pi (bridge) <--> claude.ai

  @{U}What you need@{u}

    - An ST, STE, Mega ST/STE, TT or Falcon with TOS 1.0 to 4.x, EmuTOS
      or MiNT, a screen of 640 x 200 or more, about 250 KB free RAM
    - STinG with a working network, or a null-modem serial cable
    - A Raspberry Pi, or any Linux computer, on the same network
    - A claude.ai account (or an Anthropic API key)

  @{U}On the Pi@{u}

      git clone https://github.com/whomper/ClaudeST.git
      cd ClaudeST/pi
      sudo ./install.sh --network --atari 192.168.1.20

  The installer asks for your claude.ai @{B}sessionKey@{b} (a cookie from
  the website, starting with sk-ant-sid) and runs the bridge as a
  service that starts at every boot.

  @{U}On the Atari@{u}

    1. Load STinG and check that the network works.
    2. Copy CLAUDEST.PRG, CLAUDEST.INF and CLAUDEST.HYP into one
       folder.
    3. Put the Pi's address on the tcp line of CLAUDEST.INF:
           tcp 192.168.1.10 2323
       or type @{B}/connect 192.168.1.10@{b} in the reply line instead.
    4. Run CLAUDEST.PRG. The status line shows Connecting..., then
       Online, with a green dot.

  If the Pi restarts or the network drops, ClaudeST reconnects by
  itself. For a serial cable, see @{"Settings" link "Settings"}.
@endnode

## ------------------------------------------------------------------------
@node "The window"
@{U}The window@{u}

  The sidebar on the left has @{B}+ New chat@{b}, @{B}Search@{b},
  @{B}Chats@{b}, @{B}Code@{b}, @{B}Projects@{b} and @{B}Artifacts@{b},
  then the list for the chosen area. The open conversation fills the
  right side, with the reply line at the bottom. Drag the divider
  between them to resize the sidebar; the pointer turns into a hand
  over it.

  - @{B}Title bar:@{b} the chat's name, and at the right the model chip
    (see @{"Model and effort" link "Model and effort"}).
  - @{B}Notices:@{b} short messages such as "Loading chat..." or
    "Renamed" appear in grey in the title bar for a few seconds.
  - @{B}Tooltips:@{b} rest the mouse on a name that's cut off, in the
    sidebar or the title bar, and a tooltip shows all of it.
  - @{B}Switching area@{b} empties the list at once, showing
    "Loading..." until the new list arrives. It also clears the
    conversation, so what you type next starts a new chat.
  - @{B}Scrolling:@{b} the scroll bar, Up and Down, Shift+Up/Down by
    page, Clr/Home and Shift+Clr/Home for the top and bottom.

  See also: @{"The status area" link "The status area"}
@endnode

## ------------------------------------------------------------------------
@node "The status area"
@{U}The status area@{u}

  The bottom of the sidebar shows the connection and your usage:

  - @{B}The dot:@{b} green when online, red when not (filled or hollow
    in black and white), with Connecting..., Online or Offline beside
    it.
  - @{B}The ring@{b} fills clockwise as you use up the current session,
    claude.ai's 5-hour limit: blue, turning red from 90%, on a grey
    track in 16 colours; black on a dotted grey track with fewer
    colours. Below it, "Session 30%" and, when the sidebar is wide
    enough, the time until the session resets.

  The bridge checks the usage after each reply, and at most every two
  minutes otherwise. Click the area for the full
  @{"Account" link "Account, plan and usage"} page.
@endnode

## ------------------------------------------------------------------------
@node "Chats, projects and search"
@{U}Chats, projects and search@{u}

  @{B}Chats@{b} (F2) lists your recent chats under date headings, as on
  claude.ai: Pinned first (pinned chats also show a small diamond), then
  Today, Yesterday, a date for each of the last seven days, and Older.
  The headings can't be clicked, and Tab skips them.

  @{B}Unread chats@{b} have a small dot to the left of their name (blue
  in 16 colours): a reply came in since you last read the chat, for
  example one that finished on the website. Opening it removes the dot.
  Older chats never get one: claude.ai keeps no read time for them.

  @{B}Projects@{b} (F3) lists your projects. Opening one lists its chats,
  with "< All projects" at the top. A new chat started there is created
  in that project.

  @{B}Search@{b} (F5 or Control+F) empties the list and turns the reply
  line into "Find:". Type words from a chat's title and press Return.

  @{B}New chat@{b} (F1 or Control+N) starts a new conversation.

  A long conversation shows its last 40 messages.
@endnode

## ------------------------------------------------------------------------
@node "Right-click menus"
@{U}Right-click menus@{u}

  Right-click an item, or pick it with Tab and press Insert. The item
  is highlighted while its menu is open.

    Chat           Open, Pin/Unpin, Rename..., Move to project...,
                   Delete...
    Project        Open, Pin/Unpin, Rename..., Archive, Delete...
    Code session   Open, Rename..., Archive
    Artifact       Open, Save to disk...

  @{B}Move to project...@{b} lists your projects, with "New project..."
  at the top: it asks for a name, creates the project and moves the chat
  into it.

  @{B}Rename...@{b} opens a dialog with the current name: edit it and
  press Return, or Esc to cancel. @{B}Delete...@{b} always asks first.
  In menus, the arrow keys and Return work too, and Esc closes them.
@endnode

## ------------------------------------------------------------------------
@node "Model and effort"
@{U}Model and effort@{u}

  The chip at the right of the title bar shows the open chat's model
  and effort, such as "Opus 5.5 . Med". Click it, or press F9, for the
  menu: the models, then the effort levels that model supports.

  - @{B}Effort:@{b} Low, Medium, High, Extra high and Max. Higher effort
    means more thinking: slower and more thorough.
  - @{B}Each chat keeps its own model and effort.@{b} Opening a chat
    shows what claude.ai has for it. Changing them changes that chat,
    and also becomes the choice for new chats, saved in CLAUDEST.INF.
  - New chats start on @{B}Default model@{b}, your account's own choice,
    until you pick one.
@endnode

## ------------------------------------------------------------------------
@node "Code sessions" "Code: Claude Code sessions"
@{U}Code: Claude Code sessions@{u}

  @{B}Code@{b} (F6) lists your Claude Code sessions from claude.ai/code,
  newest first.

  - @{B}Reading:@{b} your messages and Claude's replies, with each tool
    call as a one-line note such as [Bash: make -C st]. Tool output is
    left out.
  - @{B}Replying:@{b} what you type goes to the open session, and Claude
    Code's replies appear as it works. If it's still working after 15
    minutes, ClaudeST stops waiting; open the session again later to see
    the rest.
  - @{B}Model:@{b} the chip shows the session's own model. Changing it
    switches only that session.
@endnode

## ------------------------------------------------------------------------
@node "Artifacts"
@{U}Artifacts@{u}

  @{B}Artifacts@{b} (F4) lists the artifacts in your 40 most recent
  chats, newest first, under date headings. claude.ai keeps artifacts
  inside chats, so the bridge looks through them, showing "Scanning chat
  12/40" meanwhile. The next time, only new or changed chats are
  scanned.

  - @{B}Open@{b} shows the source. A Word document shows its text.
  - @{B}Save to disk...@{b} opens the file selector with an 8.3 name
    such as SNAKE_GA.PY or COVER_LE.DOC. Text is saved in the Atari
    character set with CR/LF line ends; Word, PDF and other binary files
    are saved unchanged.
@endnode

## ------------------------------------------------------------------------
@node "Account, plan and usage"
@{U}Account, plan and usage@{u}

  Press F8, click the status area, or choose Options > Account...

  - @{B}Plan:@{b} Free, Pro, Max (5x or 20x usage), Team or Enterprise.
  - @{B}Usage:@{b} a meter for each limit, as on claude.ai's Usage page:
    the current 5-hour session, this week's usage, and any included
    cloud session credit, each with when it resets or expires.
  - @{B}Account:@{b} your name, email, organization and the date you
    joined.

  See also: @{"The status area" link "The status area"}
@endnode

## ------------------------------------------------------------------------
@node "Hebrew"
@{U}Hebrew@{u}

  TOS has no right-to-left text support, so ClaudeST lays it out
  itself. A paragraph that starts in Hebrew is right-aligned and reads
  right to left, with English words and numbers inside it kept in
  order. The same applies to titles and to the reply line.

  @{B}Typing Hebrew:@{b} press F10, or choose Options > Hebrew keys. The
  letter keys then type Hebrew in the Israeli SI-1452 layout, and an HE
  badge shows in the reply box. Keys are mapped by position, so any
  national Atari keyboard works.

  The Atari font has only the plain letters, so the bridge drops vowel
  points (niqqud). If Hebrew shows as other symbols with your font, see
  @{"Font" link "Font"}.
@endnode

## ------------------------------------------------------------------------
@node "Font"
@{U}Font@{u}

  @{B}Options > Font...@{b} opens a font selector: the font families on
  the left, the sizes of the chosen one beside them, what kind of font
  it is (bitmap, Speedo, TrueType, Type 1) and a sample below. The font
  is used for everything in ClaudeST's window; menus and the selector
  keep the system font.

  - @{B}The system font@{b} comes in two sizes, 8 x 8 and 8 x 16.
  - @{B}GDOS fonts:@{b} with NVDI or SpeedoGDOS, its fonts are listed
    too. ClaudeST needs a fixed-width font: proportional ones are listed
    in grey. The first time, each font is measured ("Looking for
    fixed-width fonts" on the status line) and the answers kept in
    CLAUDEST.FNT. Delete that file to measure them all again.
  - @{B}Hebrew letters:@{b} fonts keep them in different places. The
    three buttons under "Hebrew in bitmap fonts" each show the word
    shalom as that kind of font would (Atari, ISO 8859-8, DOS 862): pick
    the one that reads correctly. A vector font is asked through NVDI 5
    for its own Hebrew letters; without them, they are drawn in the
    system font.

  Keys: Up and Down change the family, Left and Right the size, Return
  is OK and Esc cancels.
@endnode

## ------------------------------------------------------------------------
@node "Keys"
@{U}Keys@{u}

    F1, Control+N     New chat
    F2                Chats
    F3                Projects
    F4                Artifacts
    F5, Control+F     Search chat titles
    F6                Code
    F8                Account
    F9                Model and effort
    F10               Hebrew keyboard on or off
    Tab, Shift+Tab    Pick a sidebar item; Return opens it
    Insert            The picked item's menu
    Left, Right       Move in the reply line (Shift: to the start or
                      end; Control: by word)
    Esc, Undo         Clear the reply line
    Control+R         Reconnect to the bridge
    Help              This help, in ST-Guide (without ST-Guide:
                      About ClaudeST)
    Control+Q         Quit

  In the reply line, @{B}/connect 192.168.1.10@{b} (or /connect
  host:port) sets the gateway address, and @{B}/serial@{b} switches to
  the serial port.
@endnode

## ------------------------------------------------------------------------
@node "Settings" "Settings (CLAUDEST.INF)"
@{U}Settings (CLAUDEST.INF)@{u}

  ClaudeST saves its settings to CLAUDEST.INF, next to CLAUDEST.PRG, as
  soon as you change them. You can also edit it with any text editor.
  Lines starting with ; are comments.

    tcp 192.168.1.10 2323   the bridge over STinG (/connect, Options)
    serial                  the bridge over the serial port
    baud 19200              4800, 9600 or 19200 (Options)
    sidebar 240             the sidebar's width, by dragging the divider
    keyboard hebrew         F10 / Options > Hebrew keys
    port 7                  on a TT or Falcon, which serial port to use:
                            6 MFP, 7 SCC B (the Falcon's Modem port),
                            8 TT-MFP, 9 SCC A
    model <id> <effort>     the model chip / F9
    font 1 8 0              Options > Font: the font's id (1 = system
                            font), its size, and where it keeps Hebrew
                            (0 Atari, 1 ISO-8859-8, 2 DOS 862)

  A @{B}serial cable@{b}: connect the Atari's Modem port to a
  USB-to-RS-232 adapter on the Pi with a null-modem cable (never
  straight to the Pi's pins), run "sudo ./install.sh --port auto" on the
  Pi, and choose Options > Serial port on the Atari.
@endnode

## ------------------------------------------------------------------------
@node "What is stored where"
@{U}What is stored where@{u}

  - @{B}claude.ai:@{b} your chats, projects, artifacts and Code
    sessions. The bridge fetches them each time; no chat is stored on
    the Pi.
  - @{B}Pi:@{b} your session key and the bridge's options (readable only
    by root and the bridge), and the effort you chose for each chat.
  - @{B}Atari:@{b} CLAUDEST.INF (settings) and CLAUDEST.FNT (which fonts
    are fixed-width). The open conversation lives only in memory while
    ClaudeST runs.
@endnode

## ------------------------------------------------------------------------
@node "Troubleshooting"
@{U}Troubleshooting@{u}

  @{B}Connecting... never turns into Online@{b}
    On the Pi, check "systemctl status claude-st", the address in
    CLAUDEST.INF, and that the Atari's address matches the installer's
    --atari option.

  @{B}An error mentions the session key, or HTTP 401/403@{b}
    claude.ai logged you out: get a new sessionKey from the website and
    run "sudo ./install.sh --set-key" on the Pi.

  @{B}"Too many requests" (HTTP 429)@{b}
    claude.ai is limiting requests for a while. Wait a few minutes; F8
    shows your usage.

  @{B}Hebrew shows as other symbols@{b}
    Your font keeps its Hebrew letters elsewhere: pick the right sample
    in Options > Font, see @{"Font" link "Font"}.

  @{B}Something else fails@{b}
    Watch "journalctl -u claude-st -f" on the Pi while you try again on
    the Atari.

  The full guide is docs/GUIDE.md at github.com/whomper/ClaudeST.
@endnode

